# Experiment protocol: <name>

## Summary (after the run, for a person)

- **Question †:** <the Intent's question in plain words, first — the Result
  below is its answer (a person reading only the Summary did not recognise
  the Result as the answer, 2026-10-08)>
- **Result:** <!-- s --> <the answer to the question above, in at most two
  plain sentences: whether the bar set before the run was met ("Yes — the
  class descriptions kept the cable set out of the framework class in all
  five cases, as required") — or that no verdict was possible because an
  admissibility condition failed, and what was still read descriptively;
  the result, not the procedure; no intent ids, metric keys or run_ids,
  they are below>
- **Limits:** <how far it holds, one sentence — the sample, a
  single run, what was not tested; a deviation after the freeze that
  touches the verdict is named here too>
- **Next step:** <what follows — a decision, the next experiment,
  or "none">
- **Did †:** <what was done, one sentence — for a reader with no context>

## Intent (before the run)

- **Terms of this protocol:** <!-- s --> <first, because the question uses
  them: one line per house word — *everyday word · house term · code word*
  ("gap case (a case only an uncovered rule can reject) · Lückenfall ·
  `GAP_CASES`"); look up before coining (the field's established term, then
  `notes/theorie/terms.md`, then the lab standard's vocabulary table);
  "n/a — standard vocabulary" only if nothing is coined>
- **Question / hypothesis:** <!-- s --> <the one question — what do we expect?>
- **Success / failure criterion:** <!-- sm --> <how do we recognise whether it worked?
  One id per conjunct, written `[C…]` with its number in the brackets
  (C1 → the key `c1_pass`), each reappearing as a lowercase key in the reportable
  run's metrics.json; where a number stands, name its constant from the
  [[promise]] table ("in at least `min_draws` = 2 of 3 draws") — the one
  word that links this sentence, the table and the script (a person
  reading German prose and English code found no link, round 15); a
  house term used here for the first time carries its everyday word once
  in parentheses ("over the three draws (passes) of the arm (profile)
  `after`") — Terms below is the home of the definition, this line its
  first use (LAB-092 Term layering)
  (→ LAB-003 No run without an intent paragraph, LAB-004 Identifiers make the intent addressable)>
- **Admissibility condition:** <!-- sm --> <what must hold in the same run before
  any verdict is issued — yield, coverage, a control showing its expected
  value; one A per condition, each with WHY its failure makes the measurement
  uninterpretable (not merely less likely to succeed — a threshold the
  hypothesis needs is a C, never an A); if one fails, there is no C verdict,
  only the finding that the instrument failed; what is still read
  descriptively is labelled so (→ LAB-004 Identifiers make the intent addressable)>
- **Registered branch:** <if a result decides the conclusion or the next
  analysis, register it BEFORE the run: condition + what each branch
  licenses, one B per branch point — ask "and if [C…] fails, what then?";
  a stop rule may fire during the run or on an inadmissible one, so a B is
  decided on its own condition, not on the verdict (→ LAB-013 Registered branch when the run can answer two questions, LAB-004 Identifiers make the intent addressable);
  "n/a — only one outcome" when you have no answer yet: a decision taken
  after the run goes to Next step, labelled as taken after the run>
- **Timebox:** <hard budget, e.g. 4h — then log and ask>
- **Split touched:** <which split may be touched: train | dev | test>
- **Why this design †:** <choice of model, dataset, method — one sentence>
- **Rival explanations †:** <per confound: what OTHER than the hypothesis
  produces the same number · error direction (does it push for or against
  the hypothesis?) · the check as CODE in evaluate() (→ LAB-044 The check on a confound is code)>
- **Out of scope †:** <the neighbouring questions this run leaves open>

## Result (after the run — write back, don't go quiet)

- **Answer to the question:** <!-- s --> <per registered id its verdict, and the
  run_id; numbers as a small table below the label (one row per arm or
  condition), produced by `scripts/02_analyze.py --check`, never retyped>
- **Answering run:** none yet — the run `finalize` holds this protocol to,
  written with the number by `protocol-fill PROTOCOL-<name>.md --from-run`
  after `finalize`; `finalize` refuses any other run_id here (a readable
  projection of `runs.bound_answering_run`, never a second binding)
- **Deviations after freeze:** <"none", or what was changed after
  `frozen = true` — the registered plan is the Intent above and is not
  rewritten; a deviation that touches the verdict is named in Summary →
  Limits as well>
- **Raw output:** <!-- sm --> <one real prompt+answer pair per finding>
- **Interpretation / next step:** <one sentence; keep instrument findings
  apart from findings about the subject>
- **Outcome †:** <expected or surprising — failures belong here>
- **Rival check †:** <which rivals are ruled out, which still stand?>
- **Caps/drops †:** <what was omitted or limited>
- **Analysis audit †:** <date · scope sha · N findings — the audit of the
  EVALUATION against the frozen criterion, before the number leaves; at
  cost seconds due at promotion or when the number steers a decision
  (→ LAB-050 Analysis audit before reporting, LAB-017 The number of audits follows the cost tier)>

